{"kind":"propose","text":"SUPPORTED-HOST DECISION: support the resident only on a single-user or otherwise fully trusted-process host. Do not claim shared-host or mutually untrusted local-process support.\n\nCurrent boundary. gs serve accepts only TCP loopback, but loopback proves network location, not client identity. A client chooses an actor and a private session identifier in POST /v0/presence. The resident resolves that actor from repository custody and binds the identifier once; presenting it to /v0/say or /v0/act then causes the resident to sign with that actor key. Public session handles are deliberately unrelated to the bearer identifier, which protects disclosure but does not authenticate the client that first announces it. Any local process able to reach the port can therefore select any actor whose key the resident holds and obtain a genuinely actor-signed event. The fold may still judge the act ineffective, but no later verifier can distinguish attacker intent from actor intent.\n\nOption A, chosen: make this trust assumption an explicit supported-host contract. A future implementation must require an operator acknowledgement before starting a signing resident, keep non-loopback listeners refused, and surface the same warning in deployment guidance and resident status. Supported deployment means one trusted OS account or an equivalently isolated container or VM, with no mutually untrusted process able to reach the resident or read that account storage. Repository actor keys, the resident advertisement, and private session identifiers remain protected by that account boundary. A session identifier is a short-lived bearer credential, not user authentication; it must remain out of presence, logs, URLs, diagnostics, and durable events, stay bound to one actor, and expire on lease loss or resident restart.\n\nOption B, deferred: authenticated local clients could support distinct OS principals only after identity is established before actor selection and before any actor key is used. TCP loopback offers no portable peer credential. A Unix-domain or platform-native transport could bind an OS principal, but needs a governed principal-to-actor policy and a fail-closed cross-platform story. A protected per-actor capability could work only when its storage is unavailable to unauthorized processes, is scoped to one actor and repository, is rotatable and revocable, and is checked before session creation. A file readable by the same OS account does not protect against a malicious process under that account. Keeping all actor keys in one resident also makes that resident a high-value wildcard custodian. These costs are a separate architecture feature, not a flag on the current HTTP service.\n\nSigning and custody. The sequencing kernel and fold contracts do not change. The application resident remains the custodian above the kernel; signatures prove possession of an actor key, while the fold separately decides semantic force. No authentication claim may be inferred from a valid signature produced through the resident. Read-only projection endpoints do not grant custody, but every endpoint that creates, renews, uses, or ends a session is inside this boundary.\n\nCompromise and recovery. Exposure of a private session compromises that actor for the remaining lease. Execution by an untrusted process able to announce sessions compromises every actor key the resident can use during the exposure window. Recovery is to stop the resident, preserve the append-only log, invalidate all sessions by restart, secure the host boundary, audit events in the exposure window, record durable dissent or incident evidence where intent is disputed, retire compromised actor principals and local custody under normal authority, create replacement principals, and rotate sequencer or capability material when it was exposed. Recovery never deletes or rewrites signed history.\n\nDeployment boundary. Documentation and startup output must say that loopback is not authentication. Starting a signing resident without an explicit trusted-local-clients acknowledgement must fail closed; automation must provide that acknowledgement deliberately. The acknowledgement does not make a shared host safe and must not be inferred from binding loopback, running in a generic container, or file mode alone. Browser and MCP guidance must repeat that every process inside the trusted boundary can act as every locally held actor.\n\nAcceptance criteria before any future shared-host claim: (1) a process under an unauthorized OS principal cannot create or renew a session for another actor and cannot append any actor-signed event; (2) actor identity is bound before custody is touched; (3) a session, public handle, or capability for one actor cannot be replayed or confused for another actor or repository; (4) mutating session, speech, durable-act, and departure endpoints fail closed before side effects; (5) credentials are unforgeable, bounded, actor-scoped, stored outside unauthorized reach, revocable, rotatable, and excluded from logs and durable payloads; (6) restart and revocation invalidate old sessions without rewriting history; (7) unsupported platforms and TCP fallback refuse shared-host mode; (8) adversarial tests run separate principals and processes, prove that no commit or frame is emitted on rejection, cover stolen, expired, replayed, cross-actor and cross-repository credentials, and state honestly whether malicious same-account processes remain out of scope; (9) independent review verifies the application-host, resident, nexus, service, MCP, custody and deployment boundaries; and (10) the simpler single-user mode remains available without carrying an unused authentication framework.\n\nArchitecture: this chooses a contract at the application-host and outer-adapter layers; it changes no kernel ontology or signature rule. Security: it removes an implied shared-host guarantee and fails closed unless the operator accepts the real local trust boundary. Simplification: it avoids building a partial authentication layer that cannot protect against same-account processes; shared-host support requires its own ratified design."}